Question: A rhombus has an area of 168 cm² and a side of 13 cm. What is the length of its longer diagonal?
Status: Error
Explanation: Let the diagonals be d1 and d2.
Area = (1/2) * d1 * d2 = 168 cm² => d1 * d2 = 336.
Side (s) = 13 cm. The diagonals of a rhombus bisect each other at right angles. So, (d1/2)² + (d2/2)² = s².
(d1/2)² + (d2/2)² = 13² = 169.
Multiplying by 4: d1² + d2² = 4 * 169 = 676.
We have a system of equations:
1) d1 * d2 = 336
2) d1² + d2² = 676
From (1), d2 = 336/d1. Substitute into (2):
d1² + (336/d1)² = 676
d1⁴ + 336² = 676d1²
d1⁴ - 676d1² + 112896 = 0.
Let x = d1². Then x² - 676x + 112896 = 0.
Using the quadratic formula: x = [676 ± sqrt(676² - 4 * 1 * 112896)] / 2
x = [676 ± sqrt(456976 - 451584)] / 2
x = [676 ± sqrt(5392)] / 2.
Since 5392 is not a perfect square (70²=4900, 80²=6400), the diagonals are not rational numbers.
sqrt(5392) ≈ 73.43.
x = [676 ± 73.43] / 2.
d1² = (676 + 73.43) / 2 = 749.43 / 2 = 374.715 => d1 ≈ 19.36 cm.
d2² = (676 - 73.43) / 2 = 602.57 / 2 = 301.285 => d2 ≈ 17.36 cm.
The longer diagonal is approximately 19.36 cm.
